<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lhasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lhasa is the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, at an altitude of about 3,650 metres in the valley of the Kyi River. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> made it the capital of the Tibetan Empire in the 7th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> temple founded in his time became the holiest shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. From 1642 it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>' government, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> overlooks the city, and since 1951 it has been under Chinese rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lhasa.jpg" alt="Lhasa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pavel Špindler · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_-_Pot%C3%A1la_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
